<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Rot an der Rot</h1><p class="st-meta">1376 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Rot an der Rot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Premonstratensian abbey in upper Swabia, the order's first house in the region, whose abbots ruled a small imperial territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Founded around 1126 by Premonstratensian canons, Rot was the first house of the order in Swabia. Its lands grew through gifts from local nobles, and in 1376 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> confirmed its direct dependence on the empire, freeing it from outside advocates.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot governed several villages in the Rot valley as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a> and of the Swabian bench of prelates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. The abbey's income came from farms, forests and tithes. It remained Catholic and in the 18th century rebuilt its church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-verena">Saint Verena</a> in late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the abbey was secularised and given to the counts of Wartenberg as compensation, and in 1806 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a>. The church and abbey buildings survive in Rot an der Rot.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1376 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.08%;width:8.50%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1376 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rot an der Rot</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Roth Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1590&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-rot-an-der-rot" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Rot an der Rot on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4913 497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Rot an der Rot lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5233 753L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5233 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5233 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rot an der Rot. <a href="/atlas?year=1590&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-rot-an-der-rot">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

